INT. MIDWEST DINER - MORNING

Formica counters. A pie case humming. Steam on the windows.

CLARENCE VOSS-HOLT (70s, suspenders, a coffee cup he never drinks) sits at the counter. His daughter ESTELLE (40s, work blazer) slides into the stool beside him as the waitress, MARISELA TORRES (50s, apron), pours her a cup, and in the corner booth two men in feed-store caps, ROY DUNCAN (60s) and LEVI HARKNESS (60s), argue softly about corn futures.

CLARENCE
You came.

ESTELLE
You called.

He nods. She stirs her coffee. Neither looks at the other. Marisela moves down the counter. Roy slaps the table; Levi laughs.

CLARENCE
I sold the farm.

Estelle's spoon stops. Her jaw tightens. He keeps looking at the pie case.

ESTELLE
You what?

CLARENCE
Signed Friday.

She stands so fast the stool spins. Marisela freezes with the pot in her hand. Roy and Levi fall silent. Estelle grips the counter, breathes, and sits again. He finally turns.

CLARENCE (CONT'D)
Mom would've wanted me out.

ESTELLE
Mom would've wanted you to ask.

Clarence reaches over and lays a hand on hers. She lets him. Marisela sets the pot down, and without a word, brings two slices of pie.
